# Aurora Media Server — privilege grant for the in-app "Update Now" button.
#
# Allows the aurora system user to trigger the one-shot updater
# service WITHOUT a password. The updater itself runs as root via
# the aurora-update.service unit; this rule only authorizes the
# aurora user to *start* that unit, not to bypass it.
#
# Why a sudoers grant rather than the aurora binary running as root:
#   - Aurora handles untrusted input (HTTP from the public internet)
#     and shouldn't run with elevated privileges.
#   - The systemd boundary keeps the privileged code (rebuild +
#     binary swap + service restart) in a separately-audited unit
#     file, not woven through Go business logic.
#
# Install this fragment via `visudo -cf` validation, never copy
# straight to /etc/sudoers.d/ — a syntax error here locks every
# sudo invocation out of the box (including the one you'd need
# to fix it). install.sh handles the validation step.

# Allow starting the updater (covers the "Update Now" button).
aurora ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl start aurora-update.service
aurora ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start aurora-update.service

# Allow querying the unit's status (used by the API handler to
# detect "is an update currently running?" without parsing the
# status JSON's timestamps).
aurora ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl is-active aurora-update.service
aurora ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl is-active aurora-update.service
